  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A logistics manager tracks the total distance $s$, in kilometers, traveled by a delivery drone. The following table shows the drone\u2019s speed.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$** | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${s}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{(km/min)}$ | $0.42$ | $0.48$ | $0.53$ | $0.58$ |\n\nAt $t=10,$ the drone had traveled $4.6$ kilometers. Use the tangent line approximation of $s$ at time $t=10$ to estimate the distance traveled at $t=11.$  Do not round the answer.\n\nThe distance traveled by the drone at $t=11$ minutes is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nkilometers.",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $s(t)$ at the point $a=10$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =s(a)+{s}^{\u2032}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =s(10)+{s}^{\u2032}(10)(t-10) \\\\  & =4.6+0.48(t-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $s(11)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=11.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(11) & \u2248L(11) \\\\  & =4.6+0.48(11-10) \\\\  & =4.6+0.48(1) \\\\  & =4.6+0.48 \\\\  & =5.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance traveled by the drone at $t=11$ minutes is estimated to be $5.08$ kilometers.",
